Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (A1): If all data remained solely in CSV files and were loaded into a pandas DataFrame per question: every query incurs full disk I/O and CSV parsing overhead of O(n) time and O(n) RAM to materialize all n rows into DataFrame columns. By contrast, SQLite persists data once on disk (compact B-tree pages) and answers indexed point/range queries in O(log n + k) time (where k is matching rows) using O(1) auxiliary RAM via streaming cursors, avoiding repeated full-file scans.

Task A2: Design the Cleaning and Normalisation  [5 Marks | K4 – Analyse]
A2.1: Cleaning Rules & Justifications (2 Marks)
Defect Category	Deterministic Cleaning Rule	Engineering Justification
Missing Values ('', 'NA')	Apply station-grouped Forward Fill (LOCF) for short gaps (<= 2 hours); fallback to station monthly median for longer gaps.	Hourly air pollution exhibits strong temporal autocorrelation; forward-filling preserves diurnal continuity without distorting daily means.
Impossible Values (<0 or >999)	Replace readings < 0 or > 999 µg/m³ (for PM2.5) with NULL, then impute via the missing-value rule.	Negative particulate concentrations are physically impossible, and >999 µg/m³ exceeds federal reference sensor range.
Duplicate Rows	Deduplicate on composite key (station_name, reading_date, reading_hour), retaining the first valid occurrence.	Prevents double-counting identical hourly readings when network retries re-send a daily batch.
Fahrenheit Temperatures	If station IN {'Guindy', 'Velachery'} OR temp > 55.0, convert via C = (F - 32) * 5 / 9.	Ambient temperature in Chennai never exceeds 55 °C; dual station-ID and threshold checks guarantee 100% unit uniformity.

A2.2: Normalisation & Standardisation (2 Marks)
1. Min–Max Normalisation: Scales PM2.5 linearly into [0, 1] via x' = (x - min(X)) / (max(X) - min(X)). Appropriate for visual comparison across stations on a bounded 0–100% severity gauge or feeding neural network inputs, provided extreme spikes (>999) have already been removed.
2. Z-Score Standardisation: Centers PM2.5 around zero mean and unit variance via z = (x - µ) / σ. Appropriate for anomaly detection (flagging pollution episodes where |z| > 3) and comparing stations with differing baseline variances without compressing typical values when a single severe smog event occurs.
A2.3: Standard Formatting Specification (1 Mark)
⦁	Dates & Hours: ISO-8601 extended calendar date YYYY-MM-DD (e.g., 2026-10-01) and 24-hour zero-padded time HH:MM (e.g., 08:00) for lexicographical sortability.
⦁	Station Names: Stripped of leading/trailing whitespace and formatted in Title Case (e.g., 'T Nagar', 'Adyar') matching the master stations table.
⦁	Numeric Readings: IEEE-754 floating-point values rounded to 2 decimal places (e.g., 48.20 µg/m³, 31.20 °C).
SIMATS Algorithmic Pseudocode — Procedure CLEAN_AND_NORMALISE
ALGORITHM CLEAN_AND_NORMALISE(raw_rows, station_medians)
INPUT:  raw_rows (list of raw records), station_medians (map of station -> median values)
OUTPUT: clean_rows (deduplicated, unit-normalised records)
BEGIN
    INITIALIZE seen_keys <- EMPTY SET
    INITIALIZE last_valid <- EMPTY MAP
    INITIALIZE clean_rows <- EMPTY LIST
    FOR EACH row IN raw_rows DO
        SET st <- TITLE_CASE(TRIM(row.station))
        SET dt <- REPLACE(TRIM(row.date), "/", "-")
        SET hr <- TRIM(row.hour)
        IF (st, dt, hr) IN seen_keys THEN
            CONTINUE
        END IF
        ADD (st, dt, hr) TO seen_keys
        SET temp_val <- PARSE_FLOAT(row.temp)
        IF temp_val IS NOT NULL AND (st IN {"Guindy", "Velachery"} OR temp_val > 55.0) THEN
            SET temp_val <- ROUND((temp_val - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0, 2)
        END IF
        SET pm25_val <- VALIDATE_RANGE(PARSE_FLOAT(row.pm25), 0.0, 999.0)
        IF pm25_val IS NULL THEN
            SET pm25_val <- GET_OR_DEFAULT(last_valid, st, station_medians[st])
        END IF
        SET last_valid[st] <- pm25_val
        APPEND (st, dt, hr, pm25_val, temp_val) TO clean_rows
    END FOR
    RETURN clean_rows
END

Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (A2): If missing readings were dropped instead of imputed: (1) Daily averages become biased upward if dropouts occur predominantly during clean overnight hours (or downward if sensors fail during peak pollution spikes), and valid reading counts fall below 24/day. (2) Time complexity: Dropping rows and Forward Fill both run in single-pass O(n) time and O(1) extra state per station (O(S) total for S stations). Computing a median requires sorting or selection: O(n log n) via comparison sort (or O(n) via quickselect) and O(n) auxiliary space to buffer column values.

