A3.2: Daily Summary Table Design (2 Marks)
The daily summary is materialised as a SQLite view/table daily_station_summary(station_name, reading_date, min_pm25, max_pm25, mean_pm25, valid_count) produced directly via aggregate SQL:
SQL & Pseudocode for Daily Summary Production
CREATE VIEW daily_station_summary AS
SELECT station_name, reading_date,
       ROUND(MIN(pm25), 2) AS min_pm25,
       ROUND(MAX(pm25), 2) AS max_pm25,
       ROUND(AVG(pm25), 2) AS mean_pm25,
       COUNT(pm25)         AS valid_count
FROM readings
GROUP BY station_name, reading_date;

Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (A3): If the daily summary were computed in Python loops after reading all rows into memory: Python would allocate a dictionary of lists storing all n float objects (O(n) memory, ~28 bytes per float object plus list pointers) and execute interpreted bytecode loops in O(n) time (with O(g log g) sorting for g = stations x days groups). SQLite GROUP BY streams rows through B-tree/hash accumulators in native C, requiring O(g) memory (only 4 accumulators—min, max, sum, count—per group) and executing 10x–15x faster.

