Task A5: Design an AI Application for Citizen Complaints  [4 Marks | K3 – Apply]
A5.1: Lexicon Sentiment Scorer & Extractive Summariser (2 Marks)
1. Negation-Aware Sentiment Scorer: Tokenises lowercase text into words, looks up each token in POSITIVE (+1) and NEGATIVE (-1) lexicons, and checks a 1-token lookbehind window for NEGATORS = {'not', 'never', 'no'}. When preceded by a negator, the polarity sign is inverted ('not helpful' -> -1; 'not bad' -> +1).
2. Frequency-Based Extractive Summariser: Splits the complaint corpus into sentences, removes domain stopwords ('the', 'is', 'in', 'air', 'station'), computes word frequencies across all complaints, scores each sentence by the sum of its important word frequencies (normalised by sentence length to avoid bias toward run-on sentences), and extracts the top-k highest-scoring sentences.
A5.2: Rule-Based Citizen Chatbot & Limitations (2 Marks)
The chatbot extracts intent via regex/keyword matching ('pm25'/'pollution' -> air quality query; 'complaint'/'report' -> log issue), matches the station entity against the known 12-station set (e.g., 'Adyar'), resolves temporal keywords ('today' -> MAX(reading_date)), and executes a parameterised SQL query: SELECT pm25, no2 FROM readings WHERE station_name = ? AND reading_date = ?.
Limitations: (1) Fails on typos or unseen synonyms ('Adiar' or 'particulate matter'), (2) cannot handle multi-turn conversational context ('What about yesterday?'), and (3) lacks semantic disambiguation for complex multi-station comparisons.
SIMATS Algorithmic Pseudocode — Procedure SCORE_SENTIMENT_AND_SUMMARISE
ALGORITHM ANALYSE_COMPLAINTS(complaint_list, POS_SET, NEG_SET, NEGATORS, top_k)
INPUT:  complaint_list (list of text strings), lexicon sets, top_k (integer)
OUTPUT: labels (list of sentiment labels), summary_sentences (list of top_k sentences)
BEGIN
    INITIALIZE word_freq <- EMPTY MAP, all_sentences <- EMPTY LIST, labels <- EMPTY LIST
    FOR EACH text IN complaint_list DO
        SET tokens <- TOKENISE_LOWER(text)
        SET score <- 0
        FOR i <- 0 TO LENGTH(tokens) - 1 DO
            SET val <- +1 IF tokens[i] IN POS_SET ELSE (-1 IF tokens[i] IN NEG_SET ELSE 0)
            IF val != 0 AND i > 0 AND tokens[i - 1] IN NEGATORS THEN
                SET val <- -val
            END IF
            SET score <- score + val
            IF tokens[i] NOT IN STOPWORDS THEN
                SET word_freq[tokens[i]] <- word_freq[tokens[i]] + 1
            END IF
        END FOR
        APPEND ("positive" IF score > 0 ELSE ("negative" IF score < 0 ELSE "neutral")) TO labels
        EXTEND all_sentences WITH SPLIT_SENTENCES(text)
    END FOR
    SCORE each s IN all_sentences BY SUM(word_freq[w] FOR w IN s) / LENGTH(s)
    RETURN labels, TOP_K_BY_SCORE(all_sentences, top_k)
END

Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (A5): If a trained ML model (e.g., Naive Bayes or a pre-trained Transformer like DistilBERT) replaced the lexicon scorer: (1) Time per complaint increases from ~0.02 ms (O(W) hash lookups for W words) to ~0.3 ms for Naive Bayes (O(W) vocabulary dot-product) or ~15–40 ms on CPU for DistilBERT (O(W²) self-attention). (2) Memory grows from <5 KB (two Python sets) to ~5 MB (TF-IDF + Naive Bayes matrices) or ~260 MB (Transformer weights). (3) Expected accuracy improves from ~78–84% (lexicon) to ~88% (Naive Bayes) or ~94%+ (fine-tuned Transformer) due to contextual handling of sarcasm and implicit phrasing.


