B1 Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (csv + loops vs. pandas)
Dataset Size (Rows)	csv + Python Loops Time	csv + Loops Peak RAM	pandas Vectorised Time	pandas Peak RAM
1,000 rows	66.25 ms	0.12 MB	10.67 ms (6.2x faster)	0.12 MB
100,000 rows	765.68 ms	10.71 MB	49.03 ms (15.6x faster)	10.15 MB
1,000,000 rows	7,729.98 ms (7.73 s)	107.87 MB	413.21 ms (18.7x faster)	101.31 MB

Complexity Analysis: Both approaches are O(n) in time and O(n) in memory. However, csv.DictReader allocates a new Python dictionary and boxed float object per row, incurring heavy interpreter loop overhead. Pandas parses CSVs in C and executes dropna() and min-max scaling via contiguous NumPy C-arrays (SIMD vectorisation), achieving an 18.7x speedup at 1,000,000 rows.
Task B2: Engineer Adversarial Data  [5 Marks | K4 – Analyse]
B2.1: Adversarial File & NLP Stress-Test Observations
Adversarial Input	Observed Program Behaviour	Architectural Design Lesson Revealed
1. Missing Column ('no2' omitted)	csv.DictReader returns None for missing keys; raises KeyError if accessed via row['no2'] directly.	Must validate set(EXPECTED_COLS).issubset(reader.fieldnames) immediately upon opening file.
2. Wrong Delimiter (semicolon ';')	Entire line read into single key 'station;date;...'; header check fails cleanly.	Use csv.Sniffer() or enforce strict schema header validation before row iteration.
3. Different Encoding (Latin-1 / UTF-16)	open(..., encoding='utf-8') raises UnicodeDecodeError on non-UTF8 byte sequences.	Open files with encoding='utf-8-sig' and fallback error handler / chardet detection.
4. Empty File (0 bytes)	reader.fieldnames is None; min_max([]) raises ValueError if unguarded.	Guard empty batches early (if not reader.fieldnames: return []) and handle empty sequences.
5. SQL Injection (Adyar' OR '1'='1)	String concatenation (+) alters AST and returns all stations' average; '?' returns None.	Never interpolate user strings into SQL; always bind parameters via '?' placeholders.
6. NLP Sarcasm / Double Negatives	'Oh great, black factory smoke!' -> 0 (neutral); 'not very good' -> +1 (positive).	1-word lookbehind fails when adverbs ('very') intervene or when sarcasm pairs positive adjectives with toxic nouns.

